% IV. STATEMENT OF FACTS
% ===================================================================
\section{STATEMENT OF FACTS}

\subsection{Employment and Protected Disclosures}

\para Plaintiff was employed by Defendant as a Senior Recruitment Specialist from approximately May 2023 through July 2024.

\para In her role, Plaintiff was responsible for recruiting candidates for positions within Defendant's organization and worked closely with hiring managers across multiple departments.

\para On or about December 27, 2023, Plaintiff engaged in good faith with management regarding her performance and was provided a ``Coaching for Understanding'' document demonstrating her commitment to meet Defendant's expectations and improve any identified performance concerns.

\para Prior to taking protected leave, Plaintiff made protected disclosures to Defendant's management regarding workplace concerns, including but not limited to concerns about compensation practices and treatment of job applicants with disabilities.

\subsection{Protected Leave and Fabricated Performance Allegations}

\para On or about January 8, 2024, Plaintiff notified Lincoln Financial Group, Defendant's leave administrator, of her need to take a leave of absence due to her own serious health condition.

\para Plaintiff's leave was approved to begin on or about January 18, 2024, and Plaintiff commenced approved protected leave under the Oregon Family Leave Act (OFLA) and Oregon's Paid Leave law.

\para \textbf{On February 8, 2024, while Plaintiff was on approved protected leave and had no duty to work or attend meetings, Defendant issued performance documentation to Plaintiff alleging performance deficiencies and mandating that she report to the office five days per week upon her return.}

\para At the time of the February 8, 2024 performance documentation, Plaintiff had been on continuous approved leave since mid-to-late January 2024 and had no obligation to attend work, participate in meetings, or complete work assignments.

\para Defendant's own leave administrator records confirm that Plaintiff was on approved protected leave throughout the period from January 18-24, 2024 through March 15, 2024.

\para Despite Plaintiff being on approved leave with no duty to work, Defendant documented alleged ``performance failures'' including claims that Plaintiff missed meetings and failed to complete assigned work during the leave period.

\para Defendant's allegation that Plaintiff had ``ongoing performance issues'' and a ``consistent pattern of missing meetings'' during this period is factually impossible because Plaintiff was on approved leave and had no duty to attend any meetings or perform any work.

\para The fabrication of performance allegations during Plaintiff's approved protected leave period demonstrates that Defendant's stated justifications for adverse employment actions were pretextual and that the true motivation was retaliation for Plaintiff's exercise of protected rights.

\para On April 2, 2024, Lincoln Financial Group notified Plaintiff that her Oregon Family Leave Act leave was approved from February 8, 2024 to March 15, 2024.

\subsection{Denial of Reinstatement and Accommodation Failures}

\para On or about March 15, 2024, Plaintiff attempted to return to work from her approved protected leave.

\para Upon seeking to return to work, Defendant's Leave Specialist, Mary Lawson, informed Plaintiff that she would not be permitted to return to work without first completing reasonable accommodation paperwork, despite the fact that Plaintiff had worked in a hybrid remote capacity prior to her leave without any accommodation request.

\para Between March 15, 2024 and April 22, 2024---a period of 38 days---Defendant unlawfully delayed Plaintiff's reinstatement to her position following protected leave.

\para During this delay period, on or about March 18, 2024, Defendant refused to allow Plaintiff to return to work pending completion of accommodation paperwork.

\para On April 1, 2024, Plaintiff returned to the workplace; however, her direct supervisor and team members ignored her and refused to engage with her professionally.

\para On the same day, April 1, 2024, Mary Lawson threatened Plaintiff with termination unless she provided accommodation forms by the end of the business day.

\para On April 8, 2024, Mary Lawson insisted that Plaintiff formally apply for a reasonable accommodation to work from home, despite this work arrangement having been a regular aspect of Plaintiff's position and one that other employees in similar positions enjoyed without accommodation requests.

\para On or about April 11, 2024, Mary Lawson and Michelle Scott, HRBP, conducted a ``fact-finding'' call with Plaintiff regarding her accommodation needs during which they exacerbated the symptoms of Plaintiff's disability and abruptly terminated the call.

\para Following this call, on April 11, 2024, Michelle Scott sent Plaintiff an email conditioning Plaintiff's work-from-home accommodation on a demand that Plaintiff ``must improve my performance''---thereby explicitly linking the accommodation to the fabricated performance record created during Plaintiff's protected leave period.

\para Plaintiff was not permitted to return to work with her approved accommodation until April 22, 2024, resulting in a total delay of 38 days from her March 15, 2024 leave end date.

\subsection{Hostile Work Environment and Constructive Discharge}

\para Upon Plaintiff's eventual return to work on or about April 22, 2024, Defendant subjected Plaintiff to a hostile and retaliatory work environment.

\para Sarah Holland, Senior Director of Supportive Housing, instructed supportive housing managers collectively not to work with Plaintiff, thereby sabotaging Plaintiff's ability to perform her essential job functions as a recruiter.

\para This orchestrated boycott by Defendant's management made it impossible for Plaintiff to succeed in her role, as recruiting for supportive housing positions required collaboration with supportive housing managers.

\para Defendant's creation of an impossible working situation---where Plaintiff was denied access to the hiring managers necessary to perform her duties---was a continuation of the retaliation that began with the fabricated performance record created during her protected leave.

\para The working conditions became so intolerable that a reasonable person in Plaintiff's position would have felt compelled to resign.

\para As a result of Defendant's unlawful conduct, Plaintiff was constructively discharged from her employment in or around July 2024.

