\section*{FIRST CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{FIRST CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\centerline{\textbf{Unlawful Retaliation for Exercise of Protected Leave Rights}}
\centerline{\textbf{(ORS 659A.183, ORS 659A.186)}}

\para Plaintiff realleges and incorporates by reference each and every allegation contained in paragraphs 1 through [XX] as though fully set forth herein.

\para Plaintiff engaged in protected activity under ORS 659A.186 by exercising her rights to take OFLA-protected leave for her own serious health condition.

\para Defendant was aware of Plaintiff's exercise of protected leave rights.

\para Following Plaintiff's exercise of protected leave rights, Defendant subjected Plaintiff to materially adverse employment actions, including but not limited to:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1in, itemsep=0pt]
\item Fabricating performance deficiencies that allegedly occurred while Plaintiff was on approved leave with no duty to work;
\item Delaying Plaintiff's reinstatement to her position for 38 days following the conclusion of her protected leave;
\item Conditioning Plaintiff's return to work and accommodation on manufactured performance improvement demands;
\item Orchestrating a workplace boycott by instructing managers not to work with Plaintiff; and
\item Creating intolerable working conditions that constructively discharged Plaintiff from employment.
\end{itemize}

\para The temporal proximity between Plaintiff's exercise of protected leave rights and the adverse actions, combined with the fabrication of performance issues during the leave period itself, demonstrates that Defendant's stated reasons for its actions were pretextual and that retaliation was the true motive.

\para A reasonable employee would be dissuaded from exercising protected leave rights if she knew the employer would fabricate performance issues during the leave period and use those fabricated issues as justification for adverse employment actions.

\para Defendant's conduct violates ORS 659A.186, which prohibits employers from discriminating or retaliating against employees for exercising rights under ORS 659A.150 to 659A.186.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's unlawful retaliation, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer economic damages including lost wages, lost benefits, and diminished earning capacity, the full amount of which will be proven at trial but is believed to exceed \$500,000.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's unlawful retaliation, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer noneconomic damages including emotional distress, humiliation, loss of reputation, and other compensable harm, the full amount of which will be proven at trial.

