\section*{THIRD CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{THIRD CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\centerline{\textbf{Failure to Reinstate Following Protected Leave}}
\centerline{\textbf{(ORS 659A.162, ORS 659A.186)}}

\para Plaintiff realleges and incorporates by reference each and every allegation contained in paragraphs 1 through [XX] as though fully set forth herein.

\para ORS 659A.162(1) requires that an employee who takes family leave shall, upon the expiration of the leave, be restored to the position held by the employee when the leave commenced or to an equivalent position with equivalent employment benefits, pay, and other terms and conditions of employment.

\para Plaintiff's approved protected leave concluded on or about March 15, 2024.

\para Upon the conclusion of her leave, Defendant failed to restore Plaintiff to her position for 38 days, until April 22, 2024.

\para During this 38-day period, Defendant imposed new and unlawful conditions on Plaintiff's return to work, including demands for accommodation paperwork that had not been required prior to her leave and threatened termination if paperwork was not completed immediately.

\para Defendant's delay in reinstating Plaintiff and imposition of new conditions on her employment violated ORS 659A.162's mandate that employees be restored to their positions upon the expiration of protected leave.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's failure to reinstate Plaintiff in a timely manner, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer economic damages including lost wages, lost benefits, and diminished earning capacity, the full amount of which will be proven at trial but is believed to exceed \$500,000.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's failure to reinstate, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer noneconomic damages including emotional distress, humiliation, loss of reputation, and other compensable harm, the full amount of which will be proven at trial.

