\section*{FOURTH CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{FOURTH CLAIM FOR RELIEF}
\centerline{\textbf{Disability Discrimination - Failure to Accommodate}}
\centerline{\textbf{(ORS 659A.112, ORS 659A.106)}}

\para Plaintiff realleges and incorporates by reference each and every allegation contained in paragraphs 1 through [XX] as though fully set forth herein.

\para At all relevant times, Plaintiff had a disability within the meaning of ORS 659A.104.

\para Defendant had knowledge of Plaintiff's disability.

\para Plaintiff requested reasonable accommodation to enable her to perform the essential functions of her position, specifically requesting the ability to continue the work-from-home arrangement that had been in place prior to her protected leave.

\para The requested accommodation was reasonable, would not have imposed an undue hardship on Defendant, and would have enabled Plaintiff to perform the essential functions of her position.

\para Defendant failed to engage in a good faith interactive process with Plaintiff regarding her accommodation request and instead:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1in, itemsep=0pt]
\item Delayed consideration of Plaintiff's accommodation request for 38 days;
\item Conditioned any accommodation on Plaintiff's acceptance of fabricated performance improvement demands;
\item Conducted a hostile ``fact-finding'' call that exacerbated Plaintiff's disability symptoms; and
\item Created a work environment designed to make continued employment impossible.
\end{itemize}

\para Defendant's failure to provide reasonable accommodation and its failure to engage in the interactive process in good faith violated ORS 659A.112 and ORS 659A.106.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's failure to accommodate, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer economic damages including lost wages, lost benefits, and diminished earning capacity, the full amount of which will be proven at trial but is believed to exceed \$500,000.

\para As a direct and proximate result of Defendant's failure to accommodate, Plaintiff has suffered and continues to suffer noneconomic damages including emotional distress, humiliation, loss of reputation, and other compensable harm, the full amount of which will be proven at trial.

